1) Add different noise types
The code adds Gaussian noise and salt-and-pepper noise to the original image.
This demonstrates how different types of noise affect image quality.

2) Compute simple quality metrics
The code calculates the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between the original and noisy images. 
A higher MSE indicates a larger difference from the original image.

3) Linear filtering (mean, Gaussian)
The code applies mean and Gaussian filters to reduce noise. 
Both filters smooth the image by combining neighbouring pixel values.

4) Non-linear filtering (median)
The code applies a median filter to remove noise by replacing each pixel with the median value of its neighbours.
It helps preserve edges while reducing noise.

5) Compare metrics after filtering
The code calculates MSE again after filtering and compares the results. 
Lower MSE indicates that the filtered image is closer to the original image.

Reflections
1) Which noise is best removed by median filter? Why?
Salt-and-pepper noise is best removed by a median filter because it removes extreme black and white pixel values without averaging them with surrounding pixels.

2) Why does linear filtering blur edges more?
Linear filters average neighbouring pixels, including pixels on opposite sides of an edge.
This reduces sharp intensity changes and makes edges appear blurred.

3) How could we design adaptive filters to preserve detail?
We could use adaptive filters that adjust their strength based on local image information.
For example, they could apply stronger smoothing in flat areas and weaker smoothing near edges to preserve important details.
